                <article class="seasickness-guide col-lg-8 offset-lg-2 text-left">

                    <p>A shark diving trip should be one of the highlights of your time in Hawaiʻi—not two hours spent wishing you were back on land.</p>
                    <p>Seasickness is common, especially on smaller boats in the open ocean, but there are things you can do before and during your trip that may significantly reduce your chances of getting sick.</p>
                    <p>If you know you’re sensitive to motion, have gotten seasick before, or simply aren’t sure how your body will react to being offshore, a little preparation can make a big difference.</p>

                    <aside class="medical-note">
                        <p><strong>Medical Disclaimer:</strong> This guide is for general informational purposes only and is not medical advice. If you have questions about motion-sickness medication, medical conditions, pregnancy, medication interactions, or treatment for a child, talk with a physician or pharmacist before your trip.</p>
                    </aside>

